.TH PRISMSHELL 1 "2025-01-04" "PrismBASIC" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
prismshell \- PrismBASIC interactive shell and interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prismshell
.RI "[FILE] [--] [args...]"
.br
Run without arguments for an interactive PrismBASIC shell.
If FILE is given, it is executed as a PrismBASIC program; remaining
arguments are exposed to the program via
.B PB_ARGV
(see ENVIRONMENT).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prismshell
is a retro BASIC shell featuring a line-numbered editor, direct execution mode,
arrays (including multidimensional), user-defined subroutines, block control flow,
FOR/NEXT loops, and DATA/READ/RESTORE.
Lines that do not parse as BASIC are executed by
.BR /bin/sh (1)
using
.B /bin/sh \-lc
for seamless integration with Unix tools.
.P
The current implementation includes Phase 1 and Phase 2 features: arrays with dynamic sizing
(1D and multidimensional), user-defined SUBs with parameters and recursion,
block IF/ELSEIF/ELSE/ENDIF, WHILE/WEND loops, FOR/NEXT loops with STEP,
and DATA/READ/RESTORE for embedded program data.
.P
Shebang scripts are supported; begin files with:
.P
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env prismshell
.EE
.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
The following meta commands are recognized in the REPL:
.TP
.B LIST
Display the current program (line-numbered).
.TP
.B RUN
Execute the current program from the beginning.
.TP
.B NEW
Clear the program from memory.
.TP
.B SAVE \fIFILE\fR
Save the current program to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B LOAD \fIFILE\fR
Load program from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B BYE
or
.BR EXIT :
Exit the shell.
.TP
.B HELP
Display brief help message.
.TP
.B mods
Mod management commands (see
.BR prismshell-mods (7)).
.TP
.B cd \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Change directory (builtin; affects parent process).
.TP
.B pwd
Print working directory (builtin).
.SH BASIC OVERVIEW
Line numbers enable the retro editor. Entering a bare line number deletes that line.
.P
.EX
10 PRINT "Hello, World!"
20 INPUT name
30 PRINT "Hello, "; name; "!"
LIST
RUN
SAVE hello.bas
.EE
.P
Direct mode executes single statements immediately:
.P
.EX
PRINT 2 + 2
LET x = 42
PRINT "The answer: "; x
CALL Env.Cwd()
PRINT _
.EE
.SS Arrays
Declare arrays with
.B DIM
using fixed or dynamic sizing:
.P
.EX
' 1D arrays
DIM numbers[10]        ' Fixed size
LET numbers[0] = 42

DIM data[]             ' Dynamic (auto-expands)
LET data[100] = 999

' Multidimensional arrays
DIM matrix[3, 4]       ' 2D array (3×4)
LET matrix[1, 2] = 42

DIM grid[, ]           ' Dynamic 2D
LET grid[100, 200] = 999

DIM cube[2, 3, 4]      ' 3D array
LET cube[1, 2, 3] = 777
.EE
.SS Block Control Flow
.P
.EX
IF score >= 90 THEN
  PRINT "Grade: A"
ELSEIF score >= 80 THEN
  PRINT "Grade: B"
ELSE
  PRINT "Grade: F"
ENDIF

WHILE i < 10
  PRINT i
  LET i = i + 1
WEND
.EE
.SS FOR/NEXT Loops
Classic counted loops with optional step:
.P
.EX
FOR i = 1 TO 10
  PRINT i
NEXT i

FOR i = 10 TO 1 STEP -1
  PRINT "Countdown: "; i
NEXT i

' With arrays
DIM squares[10]
FOR i = 0 TO 9
  LET squares[i] = i * i
NEXT i
.EE
.SS DATA/READ/RESTORE
Embed and access data within programs:
.P
.EX
DATA 100, 200, 300
DATA "Alice", "Bob"

READ x, y, z
READ name1, name2

RESTORE          ' Reset to start
READ first       ' Re-read 100

' Read into arrays
DIM values[3]
FOR i = 0 TO 2
  READ values[i]
NEXT i
.EE
.SS User Subroutines
Define reusable procedures with
.BR SUB :
.P
.EX
SUB Double(x)
  LET _ = x * 2
END SUB

SUB Factorial(n)
  IF n <= 1 THEN
    LET _ = 1
  ELSE
    CALL Factorial(n - 1)
    LET _ = n * _
  ENDIF
END SUB

CALL Double(21)
PRINT "Result: "; _
.EE
.SH BUILTIN CALLS
The following builtins are available via
.BR CALL :
.SS Environment
.TP
.B Env.Cwd()
Return current working directory.
.TP
.B Env.Args()
Return script arguments (from PB_ARGV).
.TP
.B Env.Get("VAR")
Get environment variable.
.TP
.B Env.Set("VAR", "VAL")
Set environment variable.
.TP
.B Env.Exit(code)
Exit with status code.
.SS Terminal I/O
.TP
.B TTY.ReadLine(prompt)
Read line with prompt.
.TP
.B TTY.Write(text)
Write without newline.
.TP
.B TTY.WriteLine(text)
Write with newline.
.SS Filesystem Operations
.TP
.B FS.Read(path)
Read file contents.
.TP
.B FS.Write(path, text)
Write text to file.
.TP
.B FS.Append(path, text)
Append to file.
.TP
.B FS.Delete(path)
Delete file.
.TP
.B FS.List(path)
List directory.
.TP
.B FS.Exists(path)
Check if path exists (returns 1 or 0).
.TP
.B FS.Glob(pattern)
Glob pattern (POSIX only).
.TP
.B FS.Cd(path)
Change directory (returns 1 on success).
.TP
.B FS.Mkdir(path [, parents])
Create directory.
.TP
.B FS.Rmdir(path)
Remove empty directory.
.TP
.B FS.RemoveAll(path)
Recursive delete.
.TP
.B FS.Copy(src, dst [, recursive])
Copy file or directory.
.TP
.B FS.Move(src, dst)
Move or rename.
.TP
.B FS.Touch(path)
Create or update timestamp.
.TP
.B FS.Size(path)
Get file size in bytes.
.TP
.B FS.IsDir(path)
Check if directory.
.TP
.B FS.MTime(path)
Get modification time (UNIX seconds).
.SS Path Manipulation
.TP
.B Path.Join(a, b)
Join path components.
.TP
.B Path.Dirname(path)
Get parent directory.
.TP
.B Path.Basename(path)
Get filename.
.TP
.B Path.Ext(path)
Get extension.
.SS Random Numbers
.TP
.B RNG.Seed([value])
Seed RNG.
.TP
.B RNG.Int([max])
Random integer [0, max].
.TP
.B RNG.Int(min, max)
Random integer [min, max].
.TP
.B RNG.Float()
Random float [0.0, 1.0).
.TP
.B Rand()
Alias for RNG.Float().
.SS Time
.TP
.B Time.Now()
Current time as "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS".
.SS Array Support
.TP
.B LEN(string)
String length.
.TP
.B LEN("arrayname")
Array length (first dimension, pass name as string).
.TP
.B ARR.DIMS("arrayname")
Get number of dimensions.
.TP
.B ARR.SIZE("arrayname", dim)
Get size of specific dimension (0-indexed).
.TP
.B ARR.SET(name, index, value)
Set 1D array element.
.P
The result of the last
.B CALL
is stored in the special variable
.BR _ .
.SH MODS
Mods are PrismBASIC files that register shell commands. They autoload from:
.IP \(bu 3
.I ./mods
(project-local)
.IP \(bu 3
.I ~/.config/prismshell/mods
(user)
.IP \(bu 3
.I /usr/local/share/prismshell/mods
(system)
.IP \(bu 3
Paths in
.B PRISMSHELL_MOD_PATH
(colon-separated)
.P
Control autostart behavior with
.IR ~/.prismrc .
See
.BR prismshell-mods (7)
for details.
.SH PROMPT CUSTOMIZATION
The prompt is determined by (in order):
.IP "1." 4
A
.I prompt
mod (if present and enabled).
.IP "2." 4
A template set via
.B Prompt.SetTemplate(...)
with placeholders:
.BR ${status_emoji} ,
.BR ${shortcwd} ,
.BR ${time} ,
etc.
.IP "3." 4
Built-in default:
.BR "${status_emoji} ${shortcwd} pbsh> " .
.P
See
.BR prismshell-prompt (7).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./mods
Project-local mods directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/prismshell/mods
User mods directory.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/prismshell/mods
System mods directory.
.TP
.I ~/.prismrc
User configuration file for autostart control.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PB_ARGV
Script arguments (JSON-like array string).
.TP
.B PB_CMD
Mod command name (when running as mod).
.TP
.B PB_ARGC
Mod argument count.
.TP
.B PB_ARG1, PB_ARG2, ...
Individual mod arguments.
.TP
.B PB_ARGS
Space-joined mod arguments.
.TP
.B HOME
Used to resolve user directories.
.TP
.B PRISMRC
Override path to rc file.
.TP
.B PRISMRC_HOME
Override home directory for rc file resolution.
.TP
.B PRISMSHELL_MOD_PATH
Additional colon-separated mod search paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell returns the exit status of the last command executed.
Program exit is controlled by
.BR Env.Exit() .
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Usage
.EX
# Interactive session
prismshell
PRINT "Hello!"
LET x = 42
PRINT x

# Run a program
prismshell demo.bas arg1 arg2

# Shebang script
#!/usr/bin/env prismshell
PRINT "Script with arguments"
CALL Env.Args()
PRINT _
.EE
.SS Arrays and Loops
.EX
' 1D array with FOR loop
DIM fib[20]
LET fib[0] = 0
LET fib[1] = 1
FOR i = 2 TO 19
  LET fib[i] = fib[i-1] + fib[i-2]
NEXT i

' 2D matrix
DIM matrix[3, 3]
FOR row = 0 TO 2
  FOR col = 0 TO 2
    LET matrix[row, col] = row * 3 + col
  NEXT col
NEXT row
.EE
.SS DATA/READ with Arrays
.EX
DATA 10, 20, 30, 40, 50

DIM values[5]
FOR i = 0 TO 4
  READ values[i]
NEXT i

FOR i = 0 TO 4
  PRINT "values["; i; "] = "; values[i]
NEXT i
.EE
.SS User Subroutines
.EX
SUB Power(base, exp)
  IF exp = 0 THEN
    LET _ = 1
  ELSE
    CALL Power(base, exp - 1)
    LET _ = base * _
  ENDIF
END SUB

CALL Power(2, 10)
PRINT "2^10 = "; _
.EE
